name  = crc32
shape = compute-bound over a byte stream, almost no allocation
args  = $DATA/bytes.bin
claim = the honest expectation is a TIE with C -- mere lowers to C and the same
        clang compiles both. Any gap here is a codegen or representation cost,
        not an optimizer difference. Every implementation runs the SAME bitwise
        algorithm: no lookup table, no library CRC (zlib, hash/crc32, Zlib.crc32),
        because a table-driven reference would compare algorithms instead.
        bench_vecint.mere is the same algorithm over `read_file_bytes`
        (Vec[R, int], eight bytes per byte) instead of `bytes`: one API choice,
        and the whole gap between tying C and being three times slower than it.

# Deterministic bounds on the default region's cumulative allocation, checked
# by scripts/bench_check.sh. Not a performance threshold -- alloc_total is a
# function of the program, so it is safe to gate on, unlike wall clock.
#
# Measured 4,194,496 B at v0.1.316 for bench.mere: the file, once, on the
# `bytes` path. The FLOOR is not decoration -- it is set just under the file
# size, so a meter that reads zero, or a program that stops reading the file,
# fails here instead of passing a ceiling forever.
#
# bench_vecint.mere reads 67,109,048 B for the same 4 MiB file and is NOT
# bounded here: it exists to show that number, and a ceiling on it would have
# to be loose enough to be meaningless. The bound that matters is on the
# idiomatic path.
alloc_min = 4194304
alloc_max = 8388608

# bench_vecint.mere is unbounded on purpose (it exists to show the cost of the
# wrong byte API), but its number is still a record: 65.0 MB at v0.1.413 for a
# 32 MB array of one int per byte, 35.7 MB at v0.1.414 when Vec growth became
# in place -- the old buffers that used to be left behind are now extended or
# realloc'd. The 8x over the file is the representation; the 2x on top of that
# was the allocator, and is gone.
alloc_unbounded = bench_vecint.mere
